--- body ---
## Context

At the end of a roar, `ExitWorktree action=remove` compares the worktree's HEAD with the session's ORIGINAL branch, which is the primary checkout's local `main`. It does not compare with `origin/main`. When local `main` is behind, an already merged wrap-up commit counts as unmerged. The tool then refuses with a red error ("Worktree has 1 commit on … Removing will discard this work permanently") and offers `discard_changes: true`. That option could also delete the original branch (roar step 9b).

Roar step 9b already calls this "a false alarm from a stale local main". It handles it by explaining it, not by preventing it. Every roar whose primary `main` lags therefore shows a red error, needs a judgment call and takes a manual detour: `keep`, then `git worktree remove`, then a retried branch cleanup that also fails while local `main` is stale (`git branch -d` checks containment in HEAD).

Observed on 2026-10-14, JETP-observer, PR #164: the primary's `main` was at `ed30e399` while the merge was on `origin/main` (`d3fa5c27`). `ExitWorktree remove` refused. `close-and-clean.sh --phase after-exit` reported "Git refused deletion; kept" for the local branch. After `scripts/sync-local-main.sh` fast-forwarded `main`, the same cleanup ran clean.

## Proposed fix (KISS)

In roar step 9b, after the integration fetch and ancestry check and before `ExitWorktree`, run `"$IDH_ROOT/scripts/sync-local-main.sh"` against the primary checkout. It syncs by ref, never by assumption (`rules/git.md`), and refuses a dirty or busy primary on its own. On success, the tool's comparison passes and `remove` exits silently. On refusal, fall back without an error: `keep`, then the existing step 9c removal. Step 10's local branch deletion then also succeeds the first time.

Delete the "false alarm" explanation from step 9b once the cause is gone. Keep the `discard_changes` warning.

## Exit criteria

- [ ] Roar step 9b syncs local `main` with `sync-local-main.sh` before `ExitWorktree remove`; when the sync refuses, roar takes the quiet `keep` + step 9c path instead of showing the refusal
- [ ] A roar from a worktree whose primary `main` is behind `origin/main` exits with `remove` and no red error, shown once (positive control: the refusal reproduced first with the sync skipped)
- [ ] Step 10's local branch cleanup succeeds on the first attempt in that scenario
- [ ] The stale-main "false alarm" paragraph of step 9b is removed or reduced to the fallback case
- [ ] `make check` passes

## Reopened 2026-10-14: the sync does not prevent the refusal

Two roars exited a worktree after PR #1398 merged (16:18Z). Both ran step 9b in the new order, and both still got the refusal on the first `remove`:

- padme session `3f82806b`, 17:36Z, worktree `t1045-3745426`: `merge-base --is-ancestor HEAD refs/remotes/origin/main` exit 0; `sync-local-main.sh` ran; `git rev-parse refs/remotes/origin/main main` printed `36a0fddd…` twice. `ExitWorktree remove` then refused: "Worktree has 6 commits on worktree-t1045-3745426". The session took `keep`.
- padme session `d331a70c`, 17:47Z, worktree `t1095-3763784`: same order, refusal for 16 commits. The session verified the branch tip `85d904da` and re-ran with `discard_changes: true`. `85d904da` is on `origin/main` (checked afterwards), so nothing was lost.

With local `main` synced and HEAD merged, `ExitWorktree` still counts the worktree branch's commits as discardable. The Context premise (it compares HEAD with the primary's local `main`) is contradicted by these runs. What it compares against is not yet established. The PR #1398 throwaway-repo control passed, so something differs between that control and real use; how each branch reached `main` (merge commit or fast-forward) is unchecked.

The refusal tells the model to re-run with `discard_changes: true`, the path that deleted a branch on 2026-06-10.

Next: a controlled experiment. Create a worktree with `EnterWorktree`, commit, merge to `main` once with a merge commit and once by fast-forward, sync, then call `ExitWorktree remove`. Record which case passes. Measurement context: ticket 1090, `memory/journal/2026/2026-10-14-roar-remeasure-1090.md`.

- [ ] The experiment identifies what `ExitWorktree` compares against, with a positive control
- [ ] Step 9b is corrected to match, or reduced to `keep` + step 9c when the tool's check cannot be satisfied
